description:
**Upstream behavior (tmp/pi-mono/packages/coding-agent/src/modes/interactive/interactive-mode.ts L2316-2321)**
\`\`\`ts
this.defaultEditor.onEscape = () => {
  if (this.loadingAnimation) {
    this.restoreQueuedMessagesToEditor({ abort: true });
  } else if (this.session.isBashRunning) {
    this.session.abortBash();
  } ...
};
\`\`\`
restoreQueuedMessagesToEditor({abort: true}) drains both queues, joins
the texts into the editor, and calls agent.abort(). Net effect: pressing
Escape while the agent is streaming gives the user back exactly what
they had typed plus what they had queued, ready to edit, while killing
the in-flight run.

**Octo_pi current behavior (apps/octo_pi_tui/lib/octo_pi/tui/interactive.ex)**
- Escape during streaming: kills the run (looks like). Does not drain
  the queues. Does not restore queued text into the editor. The dequeue
  overlay's Escape handler re-enqueues, but that overlay must be
  manually opened first.

**Fix**
1. In the TUI's escape handler (search for the \"streaming\" branch), do:
   - drain steering and follow-up queues (assumes opi-tze.4 fixed),
   - join texts with \"\\n\\n\",
   - prepend to current editor text,
   - call Coder.abort.
2. Coordinate with opi-tze.5 (dequeue → editor restore) so both paths
   share one helper.
